```

In the ChatGPT desktop app, open Plugins, then Manage, then Marketplace. Add a marketplace with the source `pastdotdev/codex-plugin`, then install past.dev from its page.

### Trust the hooks

Codex runs no hook until you trust it, and it does not say when it skips one. Until you do this step, the plugin recalls nothing and sends nothing. In a terminal session, run `/hooks` and trust the five past.dev hooks. In the desktop app, open the past.dev plugin and select Trust all. The trust is shared between the two, and an update of the plugin keeps it.

### Connect the Codex plugin

```codex
$past:connect <project-api-key> <identity>
```

The project API key starts with `past_sk_`. Create it on the API keys screen of the console at https://app.past.dev. Do not use the project id or the organization's `past_mk_` management key: a plugin cannot ingest or recall with them.

The identity is the email or id that every memory from your sessions is attributed to. Recall answers as this identity, so recall stays off until you set one.

Without an audience, every reader in the project can see what you send. Add `--audience <slug>` to the connect command to narrow it to one audience from the console. `--audience project` widens it again.

Codex runs commands in a sandbox with no network, so it asks before it runs this command outside the sandbox. Approve it. To keep the key out of the conversation, set `PAST_API_KEY` and `PAST_IDENTITY` in your environment instead. The desktop app reads them from a login shell, so put them in `~/.zprofile`.

### When Codex sessions are sent

- Within a minute after the Codex process that ran the session stops, for example at the end of a `codex exec` run or when you quit the desktop app.
- After 12 hours with no new turn.
- Before Codex compacts the context.
- At the next session start, for an earlier session that never reached past.dev.

Codex can import sessions from another agent. The plugin never sends the imported part of a thread. What you say in the thread after the import is sent like any Codex session.

### Codex skills

| Skill | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `$past:connect` | Stores the key, the identity and the audience in `~/.past/config.json`. |
| `$past:status` | Shows the connection, when the hooks last ran, and what was sent. |
| `$past:cut [session]` | Prints exactly what would be sent from this session, or from the session you name. |
| `$past:ingest [session]` | Sends one session now. |
| `$past:backfill` | Lists the Codex sessions on this machine with their credit cost, then sends them when you agree. |
| `$past:search` | Searches past.dev. Codex can choose it when you ask about earlier work. |

### If nothing is recalled

1. Run `$past:status` and read when the hooks last ran.
2. If the hooks never ran, trust them with `/hooks`.
3. If the hooks are trusted and still never run, make `node` available on the path of a login shell.

### Remove the Codex plugin

Run `codex plugin remove past@past`, then delete `~/.past` if no other past.dev plugin uses it. Revoke the key on the API keys screen to stop it at once from the server side. Data that was already sent stays in past.dev until you delete it.

Source code and issues: [pastdotdev/codex-plugin](https://github.com/pastdotdev/codex-plugin).

# past.dev for Cursor

> Cursor chats become memory, and every prompt reaches the model with what past.dev recalls for it.

Product: past.dev Plugins. Source: https://past.dev/docs/plugins/cursor

### Requirements

- Cursor 3.23 or later.
- Node 16 or later on the path of your login shell.
- A project API key and an identity.

### Install the Cursor plugin

In Cursor, open Customize and add a marketplace from GitHub with the source `pastdotdev/cursor-plugin`. Then install past.dev from it. From a terminal, this command adds the same marketplace:

```terminal
agent plugin marketplace add https://github.com/pastdotdev/cursor-plugin
```

### Connect the Cursor plugin

Run this in a chat. Cursor asks before it runs the command.

```cursor
/past-connect <project-api-key> <identity>
```

The project API key starts with `past_sk_`. Create it on the API keys screen of the console at https://app.past.dev. Do not use the project id or the organization's `past_mk_` management key: a plugin cannot ingest or recall with them.

The identity is the email or id that every memory from your sessions is attributed to. Recall answers as this identity, so recall stays off until you set one.

Without an audience, every reader in the project can see what you send. Add `--audience <slug>` to the connect command to narrow it to one audience from the console. `--audience project` widens it again.

### How Cursor recalls

- On every prompt, the plugin recalls against the prompt and puts the result in front of it. The first prompt of a chat also gets the project's history.
- The plugin also serves the `past_recall` tool, which the agent calls to look something up.
- Ask mode offers no tools, so there recall comes with the prompt only.

### When Cursor chats are sent

A chat is sent when it was quiet for 30 minutes. A turn that the agent still works on is never quiet. What you say after that starts a new sitting, so a sitting that was sent is never sent again. When Cursor opens a workspace, the plugin sends the chats that went quiet while Cursor was closed. The plugin reads a chat from the moment you install it, so it has no history command.

### Cursor skills

| Skill | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `/past-connect` | Stores the key, the identity and the audience in `~/.past/config.json`. |
| `/past-status` | Shows the connection, what was sent, and the last send that failed. |
| `/past-cut [chat]` | Prints exactly what would be sent from a chat. The default is the latest chat in this workspace. |
| `/past-ingest [chat]` | Sends one chat now, before it goes quiet. |

Set `promptRecall` to `false` in `~/.past/config.json` to stop the recall in front of every prompt and keep the `past_recall` tool.

### Remove the Cursor plugin

Uninstall the plugin in Customize, then delete `~/.past/cursor`. Revoke the key on the API keys screen to stop it at once from the server side. Data that was already sent stays in past.dev until you delete it.

Source code and issues: [pastdotdev/cursor-plugin](https://github.com/pastdotdev/cursor-plugin).

# past.dev for JetBrains IDEs

> Junie sessions in a JetBrains IDE become memory, and Junie recalls it with the past_recall tool.

Product: past.dev Plugins. Source: https://past.dev/docs/plugins/jetbrains

### Requirements

- A JetBrains IDE, version 2026.2 or later, with Junie.
- A project API key and an identity.

### Install the JetBrains plugin

1. Open Settings › Plugins › Marketplace, search for past.dev and install it. The plugin is also on [JetBrains Marketplace](https://plugins.jetbrains.com/plugin/34827-past-dev).
2. Open Settings › Tools › past.dev. Enter the project API key and your identity. If another past.dev plugin already connected this machine, the page shows that connection.
3. Select Send Junie's sessions on the same page, or select Send them on the notice that the IDE shows. The plugin sends nothing before you do this.
4. Ask Junie which past.dev tools it has. `past_recall` is in the list, with nothing to turn on.

The project API key starts with `past_sk_`. Create it on the API keys screen of the console at https://app.past.dev. Do not use the project id or the organization's `past_mk_` management key: a plugin cannot ingest or recall with them.

The identity is the email or id that every memory from your sessions is attributed to. Recall answers as this identity, so recall stays off until you set one.

### How Junie recalls

Junie runs no hooks in the IDE, so recall does not come with every prompt. Junie calls the `past_recall` tool when it needs earlier decisions or work. Other agents in the AI chat can use the tool too, after you turn on the IDE's MCP server in Settings › Tools › MCP Server.

### When Junie sessions are sent

- When a session was quiet for 30 minutes, after you allowed sending.
- When the IDE quits.
- Sessions in the IDE's AI chat and in Junie's terminal are both sent.

Sessions from before you connected the plugin are sent only when you ask. Select Send history in the past.dev tool window. It shows the number of sessions and their credit cost first. Each request also carries the name and the version of the IDE, for example DataGrip 2026.2.3.

Source code and issues: [pastdotdev/jetbrains-plugin](https://github.com/pastdotdev/jetbrains-plugin).

# past.dev for Hermes Agent

> A memory provider for Hermes Agent: recall before each message, and conversations kept in past.dev.

Product: past.dev Plugins. Source: https://past.dev/docs/plugins/hermes

### Install the Hermes provider

Run these lines in the Python environment that Hermes runs in.

```terminal
pip install "git+https://github.com/pastdotdev/hermes-plugin"
hermes config set memory.provider pastdotdev
hermes pastdotdev setup
```

1. `pip install` installs the provider beside Hermes.
2. `hermes config set memory.provider pastdotdev` makes past.dev the memory of Hermes. `hermes memory setup` offers past.dev too and runs the same steps.
3. `hermes pastdotdev setup` asks for the project API key and your identity.
4. `hermes pastdotdev status` shows the connection and the last sessions that were sent.

`PAST_API_KEY` and `PAST_IDENTITY` in the environment work too. If another past.dev plugin already connected this machine, the provider uses that connection.

### What the Hermes provider sends

- Recall: the message you send, with secrets removed, as the query. Nothing is recalled until an identity is set.
- Memory: what you and Hermes said in each session, without tool calls or their results. A session is sent when it ends, when it is compacted or when you switch sessions.
- Cron runs, subagents and memory flushes are never sent.

> **Messaging gateways**
>
> The provider is off on a messaging gateway such as Telegram, Slack or Discord. past.dev recalls as one identity, so on a gateway that several people use, one person's memory would reach another person. If the gateway serves only you, set `"gateway": true` in `~/.past/config.json`.

Source code and issues: [pastdotdev/hermes-plugin](https://github.com/pastdotdev/hermes-plugin).

# Plugin configuration

> Every past.dev plugin on a machine reads one connection file, ~/.past/config.json.

Product: past.dev Plugins. Source: https://past.dev/docs/plugins/configuration

The connect command of each plugin writes `~/.past/config.json`. Only you can open the folder: it is mode `700`, and every file in it is mode `600`. You can also edit the file by hand.

```~/.past/config.json
{
  "apiKey": "past_sk_...",
  "identity": "you@example.com",
  "audience": "developers",
  "recall": true,
  "ingest": true,
  "idleMinutes": 720,
  "sittingMinutes": 30,
  "deny": ["/Users/you/clients/acme"]
}
```

### Settings

| Setting | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `apiKey` | The project API key, `past_sk_...`. |
| `identity` | The email or id that memories are attributed to, and that recall answers as. Recall stays off without it. |
| `audience` | An audience slug from the console. Without it, the whole project sees what you send. |
| `recall` | `false` stops recall and keeps sending. |
| `ingest` | `false` stops sending and keeps recall. |
| `idleMinutes` | How long a session stays quiet before it is sent. The default is 720, twelve hours. In Cursor, how long a turn that never ended holds its chat back. |
| `sittingMinutes` | How long a session stays quiet before a new sitting starts. The default is 30 and the minimum is 5. A session keeps the value it was first sent with. |
| `deny` | Path fragments. A session whose working directory matches one is ignored: nothing is read and nothing is sent. |

`PAST_API_KEY`, `PAST_IDENTITY` and `PAST_API_URL` in the environment override the file.

### Stop a plugin

1. Set `ingest` to `false` to stop sending, or `recall` to `false` to stop recall.
2. Revoke the project key on the API keys screen of the console to stop every plugin that uses it, at once.
3. Delete data that was already sent from the console, or with `DELETE /api/v1/data-points/{sourceId}`.
